{
 "$comment": "Fixtures as pure data (section 21). Arrays index by level. Conditions are declarative and evaluated against a shop-state object.",
 "$schema_notes": {
  "class": "additive | multiplicative | rule_breaking",
  "term": "footfall | conversion | basket | margin | none",
  "trigger.scope": "customer (fires as a customer passes this slot) | shop (evaluated at day start) | next_slots (amplifies same-term effects in the following N slots of this aisle) | exit (fires on exit)",
  "effect.op": "add | multiply | set | rule",
  "conditions": "list of { check, value }, all must hold",
  "ratchet": "effect.op 'ratchet' grows a stored value by effect.value each trading day and then applies the accumulated total to its term. Ratchets are the only unbounded scalers in the pool, and they are deliberately confined to Footfall and Basket: Conversion and Margin are rates and cap out, so they cannot carry a 24-encounter curve.",
  "clauses": "Extra effects a fixture gains at a level, as data rather than a branch in the resolver. Each clause is { minLevel, term, op, value, conditions? } and is applied by the same machinery as the main effect. A clause that touches a term other than the fixture's own rolls its own trigger — see ruling 8 in sim/README.md.",
  "position": "Conditions slot_is_first, slot_is_last, slot_index_min, prev_slot_class and prev_slot_term make WHERE a fixture sits matter. The ablation measured reordering the whole board at 0.0pp because End Cap was the only adjacency effect in the pool; section 2 calls order of operations the primary skill expression, so the catalogue has to express it.",
  "purity": "aisle_all_share_tag and shop_holds_at_least_tag pay for commitment. A concave payoff has its optimum in the middle by definition, so making a hedged build merely mediocre needs effects that reward being pure, not just penalties for going too far.",
  "ratchet_growth": "A ratchet may be fed per day, per sale, per sale to one customer type, per walkout, or per boss survived, and may carry `accel` which grows the per-day amount itself. Flat-per-day is linear and linear cannot carry a 24-encounter exponential; `accel` and the per-sale feeds are where the exponent actually lives. See sim/ratchets.js.",
  "ratchet_mult": "effect.op 'ratchet_mult' accumulates a MULTIPLIER rather than an addend: the term is multiplied by (1 + accumulated). The only genuinely exponential object in the pool, so there are three of them, all flagship, all expensive to keep and slow to start."
 },
